1
00:00:01,391 --> 00:00:09,878
Welcome to Europe episode two of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:10,535 --> 00:00:22,133
Today we connect France with the Benelux countries, then zoom into Monaco on
the French Riviera. Monaco needs a labelled supplemental map because GeoClick
omits it.

3
00:00:22,812 --> 00:00:26,562
Let's bring France and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:41,707
France is the broad country south of Belgium and west of Germany and
Switzerland. Spain lies across the Pyrenees; its coasts face both the Atlantic
and the Mediterranean.

5
00:00:42,361 --> 00:00:54,981
Revolutionary France helped establish the metric system. An early definition
of the metre used a fraction of the Earth’s meridian: everyday measuring
linked to the size of the planet.

6
00:00:55,633 --> 00:01:06,593
Use France as our western anchor between the Channel, the Atlantic, the
Pyrenees and the Alps. Think of a metre ruler stretched across that broad
outline.

7
00:01:07,297 --> 00:01:12,243
Let's bring Belgium and its neighbours into a closer view.

8
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:24,593
Belgium sits above France and below the Netherlands, with Germany and
Luxembourg on its eastern side. It has a short North Sea coast.

9
00:01:25,256 --> 00:01:36,390
Belgium has three official languages: Dutch, French and German. Its language
communities help explain why a small country can have a complex political
structure.

10
00:01:37,044 --> 00:01:48,213
Remember Belgium in the middle of the western coastal chain: France, Belgium,
Netherlands. Three languages make a useful hook for that small central link.

11
00:01:50,945 --> 00:02:02,369
The Netherlands lies north of Belgium and west of Germany. Its North Sea coast
and river mouths make water a constant feature of the country’s geography.

12
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:11,691
Holland is only part of the Netherlands: North Holland and South Holland are
two provinces, not the name of the entire country.

13
00:02:12,355 --> 00:02:22,293
Keep the Netherlands above Belgium. Remember the low coastal country by its
water-shaped outline, and keep Holland as a smaller name inside the larger
one.

14
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:27,616
Let's bring Luxembourg and its neighbours into a closer view.

15
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:42,045
Luxembourg is the small inland country between Belgium, France and Germany.
Unlike Belgium and the Netherlands, it has no coastline.

16
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:54,809
Luxembourg is the world’s only remaining grand duchy. Its name also recalls a
little castle, a fitting image for this small country at a three-neighbour
junction.

17
00:02:55,466 --> 00:03:06,170
Remember the Benelux trio: Belgium, Netherlands, Luxembourg. Luxembourg is the
tiny inland member, below Belgium and beside France and Germany.

18
00:03:07,411 --> 00:03:12,914
Let's locate Monaco on a labelled supplemental map, then zoom in to see its
outline.

19
00:03:22,231 --> 00:03:32,250
Monaco is a tiny Mediterranean state on the French Riviera, surrounded on land
by France. Italy is nearby to the east, but does not share its border.

20
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:45,147
Monaco’s Oceanographic Museum connects this small coastal state with marine
science. Prince Albert the First’s expeditions helped create its oceanographic
collections.

21
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:56,632
Think tiny state, French Riviera, big interest in the ocean. This labelled
supplemental view adds Monaco because GeoClick’s Europe target list leaves it
out.

22
00:04:01,806 --> 00:04:16,098
Our western chain is France, Belgium and the Netherlands, with Luxembourg
tucked inland between Belgium, France and Germany. Remember the metre, three
languages, Holland and the grand duchy.

23
00:04:16,762 --> 00:04:29,603
Monaco is the tiny Riviera state on France’s Mediterranean edge. Its
oceanographic story completes this five-place group; the labelled supplemental
view keeps its real location visible.
